# Copy to `.env` in this directory — the workspace root — and fill in.
# `.env` is git-ignored.
#
# `apps/web/vite.config.ts` sets `envDir` to point here. Vite would otherwise
# read `apps/web/.env`, and a key left at the root would be ignored without any
# error: the build succeeds and quietly serves the fallback basemaps.
#
# For the deployed site the key is not read from a file at all. Add it as the
# `MAPTILER_KEY` repository secret; the deploy workflow passes it to the build.
#
# Everything here is compiled into the browser bundle and is readable by any
# visitor. Vite only exposes variables prefixed `VITE_`, which is the guard that
# keeps server secrets out of client code. Do not put a secret in this file.

# MapTiler Cloud API key.
#
# Optional. Without it the app falls back to its keyless basemaps (Esri imagery
# and topo, OpenStreetMap streets, Esri dark canvas) and works with no account.
# With it, all three basemaps and all three dark variants come from MapTiler.
#
# Two things to do in the MapTiler dashboard before this ships:
#
#   1. Restrict the key to your origins. It is public, so origin restriction —
#      not secrecy — is what stops somebody else spending your quota.
#   2. Be on a paid plan. MapTiler's free tier is non-commercial and requires
#      their logo to be displayed as a linked image.
VITE_MAPTILER_KEY=

# Mapbox access token — a spike for comparing providers, not the shipping path.
#
# Set this INSTEAD of the MapTiler key to draw all three basemaps from Mapbox.
# If both are set, MapTiler wins.
#
# Known limitation before you spend time on it: Mapbox publishes no per-style
# dark variants, so Topographic and Street share one generic `dark-v11` and
# become identical in dark mode — the exact problem the MapTiler switch solved.
# Mapbox also requires their wordmark as an image plus an "Improve this map"
# link, which the text attribution alone does not satisfy.
VITE_MAPBOX_TOKEN=
